-- Bibli — demonstration dataset: one school year's worth of a small library.
--
-- Embedded in the binary (demo.go), which is why it sits beside the package
-- rather than in scripts/: go:embed cannot reach a parent directory.
-- Demonstration mode reloads it on a timer; `make demo` loads it by hand.
--
-- It assumes empty tables and a schema already in place: fixed ids, no DELETE.
-- Emptying is demoReset's job, which is what keeps the sentinel borrower out of
-- its way.
--
-- What is in it: 100 works and 115 copies, 12 titles having two or three;
-- two classes of fifteen pupils and three teachers; six months of loans —
-- 355 of them, 30 still out, 2 of those overdue. Enough that every screen
-- shows what it looks like in use: the lists are long enough to need their
-- filters, the label sheet fills pages, and the overdue list has names on it.
--
-- A library where a third of the books were late was a library in trouble, and
-- it made every screen look like a chase. Lateness is marginal here, which is
-- what a school that runs well looks like: seven loans in a hundred come back
-- after the day they were due, and seven per cent of what is out is late —
-- two books of the thirty, which is what the overdue list holds.
--
-- Dates are relative to the day it is loaded ('-12 days'), never fixed, so the
-- demonstration is never out of date. The rules a real database holds to are
-- held to here: a copy is out at most once at a time, a pupil holds at most
-- three books at once, a teacher borrows in batches for a lesson, and a copy
-- that is lost or withdrawn is not out. The loan period is the 21 days this
-- demonstration sets below (a fresh install starts at 14): every loan runs
-- exactly that, except six that were extended by three weeks — two still out,
-- four given back.
--
-- Every ISBN is real: each is an edition the BnF catalogue holds, and the
-- title, author, publisher and year beside it are that edition's (checked on
-- 2026-09-14). So enrichment answers and covers appear on a demonstration book
-- as they would on a real shelf. Titles keep the series name a spine shows
-- ("Boule et Bill — Bill est maboul"), and no edition is dated after the year
-- its book was catalogued. Three are 979 editions, which have no ISBN-10 —
-- the case the demonstration would otherwise never show.
-- Copies are shelved as a municipal children's library does, by kind and age
-- ("Albums 3/5", "Documentaires 6/8", "Romans 10/12"), plus "BD" and two class
-- collections; the guide's shelving example uses the same names.
-- The newest copy has no location yet, as a book waiting to be filed does, so
-- "Sans emplacement" has something to show and the tests read a NULL location.
--
-- Most tests read their own, deliberately small file: app/testdata/fixture.sql,
-- of which this is a superset — same first three books, same five copies
-- (shelved by age here), same five borrowers. Two read this one:
-- TestDemoDatasetFitsTheSchema holds it to the rules above, and
-- TestEveryScreenOpensOverTheDemonstrationData opens every page over it.


